||bib29|'--><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17876701</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="fig1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Expression of adiponectin receptors (AdipoR) 1 and 2 in MCF-7 and MCF-10A cells detected by means of RT&#x02013;PCR. After real-time RT&#x02013;PCR analysis (35 cycles, LightCycler, Roche), PCR products were subjected to 1.5&#x00025; agarose gel electrophoresis.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="eps" xlink:href="6604692f1"/></fig><fig id="fig2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>The effect of adiponectin (10&#x02009;<italic>&#x003bc;</italic>g&#x02009;ml<sup>&#x02212;1</sup>) on growth of MCF-7 and MCF-10A cells over a cultivation time of 6 days. Data are expressed in percentage of the untreated control (<italic>n</italic>=3). <sup>&#x0002a;</sup><italic>P</italic>&#x0003c;0.05 <italic>vs</italic> untreated control.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="eps" xlink:href="6604692f2"/></fig><fig id="fig3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>RT&#x02013;PCR analysis of the adiponectin effects on gene expression in MCF-10A mammary epithelial cells and MCF-7 breast cancer cells. Grey bars represent gene expression after 48&#x02009;h of treatment with adiponectin (10&#x02009;<italic>&#x003bc;</italic>g&#x02009;ml<sup>&#x02212;1</sup>). Data are expressed in percentage of untreated control (black bars, defined as 100&#x00025;). <sup>&#x0002a;</sup><italic>P</italic>&#x0003c;0.05 <italic>vs</italic> untreated control.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="eps" xlink:href="6604692f3"/></fig><table-wrap id="tbl1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p content-type="table-title">Sequences of the PCR primers used in this study</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups" border="1" width="85%"><colgroup><col align="left"/><col align="left"/></colgroup><tbody valign="top"><tr><td align="le